{
  "image_name": "0dc3f52d8853bad6f74b57a7d5a35391.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes several optogenetic strategies for targeting and stimulating specific neural populations and pathways in the brain. Across all rows, the workflow is similar: a virus carrying an opsin is injected into one region, expression is established in selected neurons, and light is then delivered to activate either cell bodies or axon terminals.\n\nThe main comparison is between increasingly selective ways of controlling neurons in region B and its connections with regions A and C. In the simplest approach, a single injection labels many neurons in B, and light over B activates local somata. More selective methods restrict expression to subsets of B neurons using recombinase- or promoter-dependent opsins. Other schemes shift stimulation from cell bodies to projections, so light in A activates B axons that terminate there, helping isolate a specific pathway rather than all neurons in B.\n\nA further strategy uses transcellular recombinase or projection targeting so only B neurons that connect to A express the opsin. The final rows show combinatorial designs with multiple opsins and two laser colors, allowing independent control of mixed cell types within one area or separate inputs from A and C converging onto B. Overall, the figure illustrates how viral targeting plus light delivery can dissect neural circuits with progressively finer specificity.",
